  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 221 AM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nNorthwesterly flow continues to be favored overhead Sunday morning, \nas troughing exits the area to the east. NBM and LREF guidance are \nconsistent that precipitation with this system will be out of the \narea before sunrise. A 60-80 kt jet streak at the base of the trough \nwill likely support a surface high in its left entrance region. GEFS \nand EC members are in good agreement that this high will be entering \nthe area from the northwest, providing northwesterly surface flow \nthrough the morning and parts of the afternoon. High temperatures \nare forecast in the 40s across the area Sunday, with lows in the \n20s. No hazardous weather is expected at this time. \n\nBy Monday morning, upper flow is forecast to be more zonal (west to \neast), lasting through Tuesday with only a few weak disturbances. \nEnsembles indicate a shortwave trough developing over the area \nMonday, but precipitation is not favored save for 14% of LREF \nsolutions which show a deeper shortwave. However, even these \nscenarios give less than a 30% chance that precipitation occurs at \nall across the Tri-State Area. As this system exits, a weak embedded \nridge is forecast overhead Tuesday. Overall, winds are likely to \nremain light, with highs mainly in the 50s both days.  \n\nEnsemble guidance shows troughing across the west Wednesday morning, \nmoving eastward into the forecast area throughout the day. GEFS\nand EC members support a low just to the west of the forecast \narea to start this period, though it is not certain how intense \nit will be. Some form of southerly flow may be in place during \nthe day, with current forecast highs in the 50s. However, a cold\nfront is expected with this system, and timing is a bit \nuncertain. Wednesday's highs could be a bit lower in the 40s \nwith a quicker passage, or higher in the 60s if passage is \nslower. Highs on Thursday behind the front are forecast in the \n40s. NBM 12 hr precipitation guidance shows precipitation is \nmost likely during the evening and night hours Wednesday, though\nstill only at around a 20% chance of occurring. This may be in \npart due to a dry airmass in place at the time, which can be \nseen looking at the 300 K Theta-e surface. Relative humidity \nduring the day is shown below 60%, with dewpoints mainly less \nthan -5 C. Relative humidities increase going into the night \nalong this surface, which may correspond to the higher \nprecipitation chances. LREF clusters show precipitation is most \nlikely in the event of a deeper trough, with possibly some \nwintry precipitation mixed in. Guidance indicates any \nprecipitation should be moved out of the area by midday \nThursday.",
